From: Sergey Strebulaev <s@vivantel.dev>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 00:51:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/15] feat: add decision track field, generalize kms beyond
 software, remove redundant guardrails
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every decision now carries a required track (product = what kms is for
and who it serves; process = how it's built) — the two were being
conflated, making it impossible to tell a mission-scope commitment from
an implementation choice at a glance. clarify/roadmap/bootstrap/steward
are scoped to work for any git-based project, not only software ones;
attribute/changelog and the git+Markdown storage substrate stay as they
are, since both require commit history to act on at all. The concrete
edits (retrofitting the 9 pre-existing decisions, generalizing
bootstrap's wording) are captured as a standalone plan, not executed
here — a separate, later action.

Auditing the existing guardrails against the same "is this actually
system-wide, or just a skill's own procedure" question found two that
were pure restatements of what attribute's and clarify/roadmap's own
SKILL.md bodies already say in full — deleted, since kms authoring
duplicate copies of its own skills' behavior is exactly the clutter
bootstrap/steward must not leave behind in a target repo either. Two
more had the same skill-name scoping in their wording but a genuinely
system-wide policy underneath — reworded instead of deleted. steward now
checks for this both on its maintenance pass and before writing a new
guardrail.

